  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a6334982dcae08e4698af5d208c769f4, type: 3}
  m_Name: Question2_StarryNight
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  description:
  - "Vincent van Gogh painted this work during his stay at the asylum in Saint R\xE9my.
    He looked out from his window at dawn and saw the countryside under a broad sky
    filled with stars and the crescent moon. Although the village scene is partly
    imagined, the painting captures his profound engagement with nature and with
    his own inner state. He used the night sky as a stage for strong emotion, imagining
    the swirling heavens as a bridge between earth and something beyond. In his letters
    he described seeing the morning star \u201Cvery big\u201D above the wheat fields.
    The cypress tree in the foreground, rising into the sky, suggests yearning and
    connection."
  - In conclusion, The Starry Night stands as both a landscape and a vision. Through
    the choice of viewpoint, the exaggerated cypress, the dramatic sky, and the heavy
    texture of the paint, van Gogh transforms what could be a simple view into an
    emotional and spiritual tableau. The technical means, including impasto, directional
    brushwork, and a vivid palette, support this expressive intent. The painting
    invites the viewer to feel the turbulence of the sky, the silent village under
    the stars, and the longing of the artist reaching beyond the visible world.
  awnsers:
